{
  "image_name": "0d8dfd1354649421bc68304c5df3c890.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how thermogenic fat cells arise and how different adipocyte subtypes are organized in mice. At the top, sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine, which activates β3-adrenergic receptors on adipocytes. This signaling appears to drive two related routes for forming heat-producing cells in white fat: existing white adipocytes can be converted or “reinstalled” into beige-like thermogenic cells, and new thermogenic adipocytes can also form through de novo biogenesis from progenitors and preadipocytes. Local autocrine/paracrine signals, hormonal cues, and fat-derived factors are shown as additional regulators. A cluster of adipocytes connected by Cx43 suggests coordinated propagation of thermogenic stimuli between neighboring cells.\n\nThe lower part compares adipocyte types in two depots, interscapular brown adipose tissue and inguinal white adipose tissue. Brown fat contains an Adipoq-high population with many mitochondria, high UCP1, and strong thermogenesis, and an Adipoq-low population with greater fatty acid uptake but lower thermogenic activity. In inguinal white fat, two beige subtypes are highlighted: classical beige fat, which is β-adrenergic receptor dependent and enriched for fatty acid oxidation, and a glycolytic beige (“g-Beige”) type, which is β-adrenergic independent and more associated with glucose oxidation.",
